Response (FULL, NOT TRUNCATED):
Good — that’s a smart place to start. Below I’ve organized a practical, attorney-focused checklist you can bring to your settlement conference and use when deciding whether to accept a settlement or push for trial. You can bring this list to your meeting with Robert Kim or I can turn it into an email draft for him if you want.

High-level checklist (quick)
- What are the realistic chances of winning at trial (and on which issues)?  
- What are the likely remedies if I win vs. if I lose?  
- What is my “walk-away” bottom line and best alternative to a negotiated agreement (BATNA)?  
- What specific settlement terms should I insist on to protect custody/support interests and housing stability?

Questions to ask your attorney about the case strength and risks
- Case merits and likelihood
  - Based on the facts and evidence we have, how strong is our case for the eviction defenses and for any counterclaims?
  - What specific elements must the respondent (landlord or opposing party — confirm which) prove, and where are the weaknesses in their case?
  - What are the most damaging pieces of evidence for us? Are any of them contestable (authenticity, admissibility)?
- Legal standards & burden of proof
  - What standard of proof applies in the civil eviction proceedings and in the custody/support matters?
  - Are there any dispositive motions or procedural traps that could end the case early?
- Evidence & witnesses
  - What evidence will we need to present at trial? Do we have required documents, receipts, witnesses, photographs, texts, audio, or expert testimony?
  - Do we need subpoenas for records or witnesses? Are any witnesses hostile or unavailable?
- Costs and timing
  - What are the estimated attorney fees, court costs, and other expenses if we go to trial?
  - How long until trial likely occurs? What interim orders might be entered (e.g., temporary possession, stays, emergency relief)?

Questions to ask about settlement specifics
- Settlement structure & enforcement
  - What should we ask for in a settlement to protect my housing, custody, and financial interests (e.g., payment plan, move-out date, dismissal with prejudice, mutual releases)?
  - If settlement requires payments or actions by the respondent, how will those be enforced? Do we need a consent judgment or stipulation that is entered with the court?
  - Should the settlement include confidentiality, non-disparagement, or mutual releases? Pros/cons?
- Custody and support interaction
  - How will any settlement affect the custody/support issues? Can a settlement be structured so it doesn’t prejudice my custody/support positions?
  - Should child support or custody issues be reserved for family court (or a separate proceeding), or addressed in the settlement? What are the risks of mixing them into an eviction settlement?
- Remedy details
  - If we settle for money (back rent or other), what exact language will prevent future claims or re-litigation?
  - If settlement includes move-out or possession terms, what timeline, inspections, and condition requirements should be included?
  - Do we want a provision requiring the respondent to vacate by X date and an agreed-upon procedure for extensions?
- Fees and costs allocation
  - Will the settlement include payment of my attorney fees or the other side’s? If not, can we negotiate a fee-shifting provision?
- Credits, offsets, and counterclaims
  - Are there offsets or counterclaims (e.g., habitability issues, security deposit disputes) we should ensure are accounted for in the settlement?
  - Should we consider an agreed rent credit, repair agreement, or escrow arrangement instead of immediate payment?

Questions about risks and consequences of accepting vs. going to trial
- Consequences of settlement
  - If we accept a settlement, what are the short- and long-term effects (court record, eviction on public record, impact on custody/support, credit)?
  - Would acceptance bar future claims (release language)? Will it be “with prejudice”?
- Consequences of rejecting settlement
  - If we reject this offer and lose at trial, what is the worst realistic outcome? What additional costs or harms will result?
  - If we go to trial and win only a portion (e.g., partial damages), what are enforcement implications?
- Appealability and future litigation
  - If we lose at trial, what are our appellate options and the likely chance of success?
  - Does settling now foreclose appeals or other relief?

Trial preparation & logistics to confirm
- What will our trial day look like? How long? Where is the civil division courtroom? (You mentioned Hennepin County — confirm division and courtroom specifics.)
- Who will testify and in what order? Do we need exhibits pre-marked and exchanged?
- Are there pretrial deadlines (exhibit lists, witness lists, motions in limine) and when are they?
- Do we need to conduct additional discovery (document requests, depositions) before the settlement conference/trial?
- If custody/support issues require separate filings or hearings, what’s the timeline and how do we coordinate both tracks?

Negotiation strategy and settlement conference tactics
- Strategy & bargaining
  - What is our opening position and our ideal outcome vs. minimum acceptable terms?
  - Should we make the first offer or let the respondent propose? What concessions are acceptable?
  - Any non-negotiables (e.g., keeping custody schedule intact, no eviction record, no admission of wrongdoing)?
- Signals & concessions
  - What small concessions can we offer to get meaningful gains (payment plan, short deadline, mutual non-disparagement)?
  - How will we test the other side’s seriousness (time-limited offer, request for written terms)?
- If mediator or settlement conference fails
  - If settlement conference fails, what’s next (scheduling for trial, interim motions)?
  - Can we preserve settlement talks or reopen talks before trial?

Practical items to bring to settlement conference
- Copies of all lease/contract documents, notices (eviction notices), payment records, bank records, texts/emails, photographs, repair requests, receipts, and any previous court filings and orders.
- A one-page timeline of events and a list of proposed witnesses with contact info.
- A short written list of settlement terms you would accept and your walk-away bottom line.
- Any court scheduling or pretrial order documents.

Checklist to evaluate any settlement offer you receive
- Does the settlement:  
  - Resolve all pending claims and potential related claims?  
  - Include clear enforcement mechanisms and deadlines?  
  - Address custody/support either explicitly or reserve it clearly?  
  - Avoid admitting liability (if that matters)?  
  - Include attorney fees/costs allocation?  
  - Specify whether dismissal is with or without prejudice?  
  - Protect housing stability or include relocation assistance if applicable?  
  - Provide for record sealing or notate eviction consequences (if possible)?
- Translate offer into net practical consequences: immediate money exchanged, timeline for move/eviction, effect on living arrangements, effect on custody/support orders, effect on credit.

Questions to ask now to prepare Robert for the conference
- Do you recommend focusing settlement on housing only and keeping custody/support for family court? Why or why not?
- What discovery/motions should we file immediately to strengthen our position before the settlement conference?
- What exhibits/witnesses do you recommend we emphasize at the conference to increase settlement value or pressure?
- What is your recommended range for an offer/counteroffer and our final walk-away point?

How I can help next
- I can draft an email to your attorney Robert Kim listing these questions and asking for his recommendations and deadlines.  
- I can convert the above into a one-page cheat sheet for the settlement conference.  
- If you give me the current settlement offer or the major facts (amount owed, lease terms, any prior court orders, custody facts), I can help you evaluate tradeoffs and draft possible settlement language to discuss with your lawyer.

Final note
This is a complex interplay between civil eviction procedure and family law (custody/support). Your attorney is the right person to give legal advice about probabilities and strategy — use the questions above to make sure you get clear answers on odds, enforcement, and practical consequences before deciding whether to settle or go to trial.

Would you like me to draft a concise email to Robert Kim with the key questions and requested next steps? If so, tell me whether you want a formal or brief tone and include any facts you want highlighted (current offer, deadlines, additional concerns).

