Response (FULL, NOT TRUNCATED):
Good — I can help you get organized and make sure you ask Robert Kim the right questions so you can decide whether to accept a settlement or go to trial. Below is a focused checklist you can use in a meeting or call, plus suggested follow-up steps and a short template if you want me to draft an email to him.

1) Quick checklist to bring to the settlement conference
- Copies of the complaint, answers, and all recent filings (motions, notices).
- Any written settlement offers or counteroffers (yours and theirs).
- Financial documents (pay stubs, bank statements, proof of support payments or arrears).
- Documents showing living arrangements, notices to vacate, repairs, communications with respondent.
- Any records related to custody/support (court orders, payment history, child-related expenses).
- List of potential witnesses and exhibit list.

2) Questions to ask Robert Kim — required information about case strength and risks
a. Case assessment
- How strong is our case on the eviction claims and on the intertwined custody/support issues? What are the key legal and factual weaknesses the respondent will try to exploit?
- What outcome do you realistically expect at trial for the eviction claim? For any related custody/support issues?
- What are the best- and worst-case outcomes (including monetary exposure, eviction, and custody/support impacts)?

b. Risks and probabilities
- Based on your experience in Hennepin County civil court, what is the chance we win at trial? What are the chances of partial wins or reduced relief?
- What are the likely ranges for damages, costs, or arrears awards if we go to trial and lose/partially lose?

c. Settlement vs. trial tradeoffs
- What are the main advantages of settling now versus going to trial (cost, time, stress, confidentiality, control of outcome)?
- What are the major disadvantages of settling (e.g., giving up defenses, payment obligations, effect on custody)?
- What would you recommend given my goals (stay in housing, minimize payments, protect custody, preserve record)?

3) Questions about settlement terms to clarify or negotiate
- If we settle, what specific terms should we insist on? (Examples: timeline to move, payment plan, dismissal with prejudice, attorney’s fees, release of claims, no admission of liability.)
- Can we include terms protecting custody/support status (e.g., no admission that affects custody determinations, no reporting to agencies)?
- Can we include confidentiality or sealing language for the settlement terms or dismissal?
- How will enforcement work if the respondent violates settlement terms? Can we include default provisions that are easy to enforce?
- Will the settlement resolve only the eviction or also address custody/support, or will separate agreements be needed?

4) Costs, timing, and procedural questions
