Good — I’ll give you a structured checklist and concrete questions to bring to Robert Kim so you can decide whether to accept a settlement or push to trial. I’ll also include a short decision framework and a practical checklist of documents to have ready for the settlement conference.

Main things to decide before the conference
- Your goals and priorities (rank them): keep the home / avoid eviction record / money (back rent, moving costs) / quick resolution / protect custody/child-support position / avoid trial stress/cost.  
- Your “bottom line” (the minimum acceptable settlement) and your “walk-away” (what would make you insist on trial).  
- Whether you want confidentiality or public record of the settlement.

Questions to ask Robert Kim about case strength and risks
- Based on the evidence we have, what are the strongest arguments in our favor and the most vulnerable points?  
- How do the relevant Minnesota/Hennepin County laws and court practices apply to our facts (eviction standards, defenses, procedural bars)?  
- What are the likely outcomes at trial (best, worst, most-likely) and the probability you assign to each?  
- If we win at trial, what is the realistic remedy (possession only, monetary judgment, attorneys’ fees)? If we lose, what are the likely consequences (eviction, money owed, costs)?  
- How likely is it that we can enforce a judgment (if we win) or collect money from the respondent?  
- What defenses or motions could we raise pre-trial that might change the leverage (procedural defects, discovery issues, counterclaims)?

Questions about costs, timeline, and practical impacts
- What are the estimated total costs of trial (your fees, filing fees, expert fees, time) versus settlement?  
- How long will the case take if we go forward with trial (discovery schedule, pretrial motions, trial date)?  
- What are the non-monetary costs (time, stress, effect on custody proceedings, exposure of private info) of trial?  
- If we settle now, how soon is the resolution final and enforceable?

Questions about the settlement terms to evaluate offers
- If an offer is made, what are the critical terms I should insist on? (examples: move-out date, rent arrears amount, payment schedule, possession terms, waiver of eviction record, attorney-fee provisions, costs, release of claims)  
- Should we require a mutual release of claims? Should the release be limited (e.g., only landlord-tenant claims) or broad (including custody/support)?  
- Should the settlement include a confidentiality clause, and what scope/penalties for breach should it have?  
- How should enforcement be structured if the opposing party breaches (liquidated damages, automatic entry of judgment, escrow of funds)?  
- If settlement includes a monetary payment, should funds be placed in escrow or paid through the court? What proof of payment will be required?  
- Will the settlement require court approval? Who will file dismissal or stipulation, and what language should be used to protect my interests?

Questions specific to eviction consequences and records
- Will the settlement avoid an eviction judgment being entered on public records or tenant screening databases? If not, can we negotiate language that prevents reporting?  
- If the settlement calls for move-out, can we negotiate a neutral reference or an agreement not to report to credit bureaus/tenant-screening companies?  
- If vacating, can we get time or relocation assistance included?

Questions about custody and support intersections
- How will a settlement in the eviction case affect the custody and support issues (if at all)? Could admission or dismissal of claims affect custody proceedings?  
- Should we coordinate settlement language with anything we need for custody/support (e.g., timelines, admissions, statements about living conditions)?  
