Good — I can help you get organized and make the most of your settlement conference with Robert Kim. Below are focused questions to take to your attorney, a checklist of documents and prep items to bring to the conference, a short decision framework to evaluate offers, and a sample email/script you can use to contact your lawyer or to use in the conference. Tell me if you want me to draft the actual email to Robert Kim or build a one-page summary you can hand to him.

Top priorities (ask these first)
- What is our realistic best-case and worst-case outcome at trial (monetary judgment, eviction, custody/support outcomes)? What’s the probability you place on each?
- What is the most you should accept in a settlement and the least you should offer (numbers and non-monetary terms)?
- What are the risks and costs of going to trial (legal fees, court costs, time to resolution, emotional/parenting disruption)?
- If I accept a settlement, how will that affect the custody and support issues? Could a settlement be used against me in family court?
- If we reject a settlement and lose at trial, what are the practical consequences (eviction timeline, wage garnishment, credit, enforcement difficulties)?
- What terms should be non-negotiable for me (e.g., move-out date, cash payment, confidentiality, no admission of liability, release language)?
- Can we negotiate terms that limit downstream effects—e.g., specify that settlement is not an admission and cannot be used in custody/support proceedings?

Detailed questions to ask your attorney
Case strength & trial likelihood
- What are the strongest facts and evidence we have? What are the respondent’s strongest arguments?
- How credible do you think our witnesses will appear? Do we need additional witnesses or evidence?
- What legal defenses or procedural problems could lead to dismissal or a favorable ruling?

Settlement value & strategy
- Based on similar cases you’ve handled in Hennepin County, what settlement amounts or terms are typical?
- What non-monetary concessions could increase the value for me (e.g., extra time to move, reduction of past-due rent, no negative reporting to credit/landlord databases)?
- Should we present a demand/counteroffer now? What should it say and what’s our walk-away point?

Costs, timing & logistics
- How long until trial if settlement fails? What are next deadlines (discovery, motions, pretrial)?
- Estimate additional attorney fees and court costs if we go to trial.
- How long would it take to enforce a judgment if we win, or to get possession if we lose?

Custody & support interplay
- Could court findings in this eviction case be used in custody or child support proceedings? How can we minimize that risk?
- Should we coordinate with a family law attorney before agreeing to any terms that mention children, support, or housing?
- Can we structure a settlement so it explicitly does not affect custody/support orders?

Terms, enforceability & protections
- What should be included in a settlement agreement to protect me: release of claims, confidentiality, non-disparagement, no admission of liability, payment schedule, default remedies?
- How do we ensure the settlement is enforceable? Will it be filed as a stipulated judgment?
- If the respondent fails to comply with settlement terms (e.g., payment or move-out), how quickly can we enforce? What remedies (eviction resume, contempt, garnishment)?

Practical finances & consequences
- If we accept settlement, will it avoid a money judgment or still result in a judgment on record?
- Could this settlement or judgment affect my credit, housing reference, ability to get housing, or support calculations?
- If moving is required, can settlement provide relocation assistance or time?

Negotiation tactics & court procedure
- What approach should I take at the settlement conference (e.g., firm walk-away lines, prioritize timing vs money)?
- How does the civil settlement conference in Hennepin County typically run? Is mediation available through the court?
- If the judge/mediator pushes for an agreement, what parts of any proposed deal should I refuse to sign without consulting you?

Appeals & future litigation
- If we accept or reject, what is the effect on my right to appeal later? Does a settlement waive appeals?
- If we accept a settlement, can we preserve the option to raise custody/support issues elsewhere?

Checklist — documents and prep to bring to settlement conference
- Chronological timeline of events (rent payments, notices, communications)
- Lease agreement and any amendments
- Rent ledger, payment receipts, bank statements, cancelled checks
- Notices served (eviction notice, breach notices) and proof of service
- All written communications (texts, emails, letters) with the respondent
