- What is the likely outcome at trial (win/lose/magnitude of relief) and the basis for that probability (evidence, law, judge/jurisdictional tendencies)?
- How do the custody and support issues affect the eviction case (if at all) — could settlement here affect those family-law outcomes?
- Are there any procedural or evidentiary risks (e.g., missing deadlines, weak evidence, hearsay, or admissibility problems) that make trial riskier for us?

Questions about the proposed settlement terms (if/when an offer is made)
- What exactly does the offer provide? (Get precise language: money amounts, payment schedule, move-out dates, who pays which costs, whether there is a release of claims.)
- Is the settlement global (resolves eviction and related custody/support claims) or limited to the eviction only?
- Does the agreement include a mutual release or only a one-sided release? What claims are waived?
- Will the settlement be confidential? If so, what scope? If not, will it become a public court record?
- Does the settlement include an admission of liability? (If so, think about long-term consequences.)
- What happens if the other party breaches the settlement — what remedies or penalties are included (liquidated damages, attorney fees, automatic judgment)?
- Are there non-monetary obligations (e.g., move-out by X date, repairs, non-disparagement) and how will compliance be verified?
- Will the landlord report an eviction judgment to credit/tenant screening services if we settle? Can we negotiate to prevent that?

Logistics, enforcement, and practical consequences
- If we lose at trial or accept a stipulated judgment, what is the typical timeframe for execution (writ of recovery/eviction) in Hennepin County?
- Is there a way to get a stay or time to move if we lose/settle? How long?
- Could a settlement affect custody/support hearings, child placement, or financial orders? (E.g., loss of housing stability could affect custody.)
- How will payment terms be enforced? Can we get a payment plan that avoids eviction if we default briefly?
- If we agree to move out, can the settlement include protections like return of security deposit, repairs, or replacement housing assistance?
- Will the settlement prevent future landlord claims (re-renting, new damages)?

Costs, timeline, and risk tradeoffs
- What are the projected additional attorney fees, court costs, expert fees, and other expenses to go to trial?
- What is our “best alternative to a negotiated agreement” (BATNA) — i.e., what is your recommendation if we don’t settle?
- Help me run the numbers: estimate the expected value of going to trial vs settlement. (You can ask the lawyer to assign probabilities and monetary figures so you can compare.)
- What is the timeline for trial vs continuing to negotiate? How soon would a trial be scheduled in Hennepin County?

Strategy and negotiation questions
- What are our negotiation priorities and what concessions are acceptable (e.g., move date flexibility, payment plan, limited release)?
- What bargaining chips can we use (documents, witnesses, weaknesses in landlord/respondent’s case)?
- Should we make a counteroffer now, or wait for the settlement conference to see what they offer?
- Would a partial settlement (resolving the eviction but preserving family-law claims) make sense?
- Should settlement terms be incorporated into a court order (stipulated judgment) or left as a separate contract? (Which is easier to enforce?)

Custody and support-specific questions
- Could eviction or housing instability be used against me in custody decisions? If so, how should that be addressed in a settlement?
- If settlement requires moving or changing residence, how do we protect parental time/placement? Can we include guardianship/visitation protections in the agreement or file emergency family court motions?
- Does resolving housing or money issues improve our position in family court (e.g., showing cooperation, stability), or could settlement be viewed negatively?

Trial preparation and evidence
- What evidence and witnesses are essential to succeed at trial (documents, receipts, photos, text messages, witnesses)?
- What should I bring to the settlement conference to strengthen our position?
- If we decide to go to trial, what additional discovery or experts do we need and how long will that take?

Drafting and signing
- Can you draft/approve any settlement language before I sign? (Never sign a settlement without counsel review.)
- Ask for specific protective clauses: mutual release, no admission, enforcement remedies, attorney fees if breached, confidentiality clause, and a clause limiting reporting to tenant screening/credit bureaus.
- If settlement involves money paid to the other side, can payment be structured through the court or escrow to confirm performance?

Practical “day-of” settlement conference checklist
- Bring: all evidence (radiating receipts, photos, texts), a written timeline of events, copy of proposed settlement terms, a list of witnesses, a two-line “ask” statement (what we want and why).
- Ask the lawyer to role-play possible offers and counters so you’re not surprised.
- Decide in advance: your walk-away bottom line and what you can sign immediately vs what requires further review.

How to evaluate a settlement offer (simple decision framework)
- Quantify: dollar value of the offer, likely costs of trial, and non-monetary value (time, stress, housing stability).
- Estimate probabilities: lawyer’s estimate of win/loss and likely award if you win.
- Expected value formula (rough): EV = P(win) × Value_if_win + P(lose) × Value_if_lose − Costs_to_go_to_trial. Compare EV to the settlement value.
- Factor in non-monetary elements: urgency of housing, children’s stability, and risk tolerance.

Red flags — when to be cautious about a settlement
- Vague terms (no dates, no enforcement, unspecified releases).
- Offers that require an admission of fault or waive unrelated future claims.
- No remedy for breach or no indemnity for future claims.
- Offer that is time-pressured to “sign now” without review.
- Settlement that worsens your position in family court or makes housing unavailable.

Follow-up items to ask your attorney to do
- Draft or review final settlement language and confirm enforceability in Hennepin County.
- Run the EV calculation with concrete numbers and probabilities.
- Confirm eviction enforcement timelines and whether a sealed/stipulated order is possible.
- Explain how a settlement affects custody/support proceedings and propose protective language if needed.
- Prepare the documents and witness list for the settlement conference or trial.

If you want, I can:
- Turn these into a one-page checklist you can bring to the settlement conference
- Draft an email you can send to Robert Kim summarizing these questions and asking for his recommendation
- Help you run the expected-value comparison if you provide offer numbers and your estimate of win probability

