Good — I can help you organize this so you go into the settlement conference and any follow-ups prepared and focused. Below are (A) the high‑priority questions to ask Robert Kim before the settlement conference, (B) secondary questions and things to confirm during/after a proposed settlement, (C) evidence and documents to bring, (D) negotiation strategy prompts (your “bottom line” and tradeoffs), and (E) a short sample email you can send to Robert asking to review these before the conference. Tell me if you want me to draft and send that email for you.

A. High‑priority questions to ask your attorney (must cover before settlement conference)
1. Case assessment and odds
   - Based on the facts and law in Hennepin County civil division, what is our realistic chance of winning at trial on the eviction claim?
   - What are the strengths and weaknesses of the respondent’s case and ours?

2. Consequences of settlement vs. trial
   - If we settle, what concrete outcomes can we reasonably secure (time to vacate, payment plan, dismissal, no eviction record, confidentiality, etc.)?
   - If we go to trial and win, what remedies are likely (e.g., damages, stay to arrange housing)? If we lose, what are the likely consequences (eviction, judgment, costs)?
   - How would a settlement or judgment affect the custody and support issues? Could admissions or settlement terms be used against me in the custody/support matter?

3. Enforcement and record
   - If we get a settlement or consent judgment, how will it be enforced and what are the remedies for breach?
   - Will the settlement avoid an eviction on my record or a judgment that harms housing/credit? If not, can we negotiate language to limit that harm (e.g., vacating, sealed records, withholding filing of judgment until conditions)?

4. Timing and logistics
   - What happens at the settlement conference (who will attend, how long, is a mediator used, can we request a continuance)?
   - If the conference fails, how soon is trial and what are preparation steps/deadlines?

5. Costs and fees
   - What will you (Kim & Associates) charge for going to trial vs. completing a settlement? What costs (filing, expert, depositions) should I expect?
   - Are attorney fees recoverable if we prevail, or can we negotiate fee shifting in a settlement?

