Good — I can help you get organized and ready for the settlement conference and any decision about trial. Below I’ve put together:

- A prioritized list of questions to ask Robert Kim (grouped by topic),  
- A short decision checklist to help decide whether to accept a settlement offer,  
- A practical pre-conference evidence checklist and next steps.

Use or adapt these in your meeting with Robert at Kim & Associates.

Questions to ask your attorney (grouped)

A. Case merits & likely outcomes
1. Based on the facts and the filings, what is our realistic chance of winning at trial on the eviction claims? On any related claims the respondent might raise?  
2. How would the judge likely view the core facts (nonpayment, lease breach, notice compliance, habitability issues, etc.)?  
3. How would proceeding to trial affect the custody and support matters? Could anything that happens in the eviction case (judgment, findings, testimony) be used in the custody or support proceedings?

B. Settlement strategy & terms
4. What settlement terms should we try to obtain (possession date, move-out time, payment plan, back rent forgiven or reduced, dismissal with prejudice, attorney fees paid, etc.)?  
5. What minimum terms would make me accept a settlement (your “walk-away” / bottom line)? What is your recommended target?  
6. Should we insist on confidentiality, no-admission language, and a mutual release of claims? Will settlement be binding and enforceable?  
7. Can settlement terms protect my position in the custody/support case (for example, include explicit language that settlement is not an admission and will not affect parental-responsibility claims)?  
8. If the respondent breaches the settlement, what enforcement options do we have (motion to enforce, contempt, re-filing)? What remedies are fastest or easiest?

C. Financial & collateral consequences
9. What are the likely monetary outcomes (judgment amount, costs, possible attorney-fee awards) from trial versus a settlement?  
10. How will a judgment (or eviction record) affect housing, credit, or any support calculations? Can we negotiate to avoid an eviction on record or to seal/withdraw filings?  
11. What are the expected additional costs (your legal fees, court costs, moving costs, lost wages) for going to trial and for potential appeals?

D. Procedural & timing
12. What exactly will happen at the settlement conference? Who attends? Is it confidential/privileged? Will offers made there be used if we go to trial?  
13. If settlement fails at the conference, what are the next deadlines (trial date, witness lists, exhibits, pretrial statements)? How much time do we have to prepare?  
14. Are there opportunities for mediation or other ADR before trial? Do you recommend mediation?

E. Evidence & witnesses
15. Which documents or witnesses are most important for trial? Which are persuasive at settlement?  
16. Do we need expert witnesses (e.g., housing/habitability expert, financial expert)? What would they cost and how helpful would they be?  
17. If children or custody issues will be discussed, what evidence or witnesses could link housing/outcome to custody/support?

F. Risks, appeals & bigger picture
18. What are the biggest risks if we go to trial (losing the case, adverse findings used elsewhere, higher financial exposure)?  
19. If we lose at trial, what are the appeal prospects and timeline? Are appeals practical in this case?  
20. How will an eviction judgment be enforced (writ of recovery, sheriff eviction timeline)? What’s the likely timeline for actual move-out?

G. Communication & next steps with you and opposing party
21. What is the best way for me to respond to a settlement offer during the conference (accept, counter, take time)? Should I give authority to you to accept up to X terms, or do you want to consult me live?  
22. What documents or statements should I avoid saying or sharing during the conference that could hurt us later?

Decision checklist: when to accept a settlement vs push for trial

Accept settlement if most of these are true:
- It meets your essential needs (reasonable move-out timeline, manageable payment terms, no excessive money judgment), AND  
- Includes favorable/necessary protections (no admission, confidentiality, release language that protects custody position), AND  
- The financial and emotional costs and time of trial outweigh the likely additional benefit, AND  
- The settlement protects you from adverse effects on custody/support, or at least does not worsen your position.

Push for trial if:
- Settlement is clearly worse than the likely trial outcome (based on attorney assessment), AND  
- You have strong, well-documented evidence and witnesses, AND  
- You are prepared for the timeline, costs, and possible appeals, AND  
- There are important precedent or principle reasons to proceed (e.g., clearing your record/rights).

Practical evidence & preparation checklist to bring to the conference
- Lease or rental agreement, written notices (14/30-day notices, eviction notices) and dates.  
- All correspondence with the respondent and landlord (texts, emails, letters).  
- Rent payment records (bank statements, receipts, ledgers).  
- Photos/videos of unit condition, repair requests, inspection reports.  
- Police reports, 911 calls, or incident reports if relevant.  
- Any previous court orders or filings in this or related family court files (custody/support).  
- Names and contact info for likely witnesses (neighbors, repair people, others).  
- A short chronology/timeline of events (one page) you can give to Robert.  
- Budget estimate of your costs (moving, storage, legal fees) to use in negotiations.  
- Proposed settlement terms you’d accept (in writing), and your non-negotiables.

Suggested phrasing to use with your attorney during the conference
- “My priorities are X, Y, Z. I authorize you to accept any settlement that meets these conditions; otherwise please consult me.”  
- “I will not accept a settlement that does A, B, or C (list non-negotiables).”  
- “Can we insert a clause saying the settlement will not be used in the custody/support case and is not an admission of liability?”

Other practical questions to ask Robert now
- Do you need any additional documents or witnesses from me before the conference?  
- How should I act and what should I say/not say during the conference?  
- If we reach a tentative deal at the conference, how soon will it be put in writing and signed? Will it be immediately enforceable?

Final suggestions
- Before the conference, set your BATNA (best alternative to negotiated agreement)—what you will do if there is no acceptable settlement. Share that with Robert so he can negotiate effectively.  
- Ask Robert to role-play likely offers and walk you through responses so you’re not making decisions under pressure.  
- Get any settlement in writing and reviewed thoroughly before signing.

If you want, I can:
- Turn these into an email you can send to Robert Kim asking for a pre-conference meeting or answers, or  
- Draft a one-page timeline and “my settlement bottom line” template you can print and bring to the conference.

Which would you prefer (email to Robert, one-page timeline, or both)?

