Response (FULL, NOT TRUNCATED):
Good — I can help you get organized and ready for the settlement conference so you can make an informed decision with your lawyer about whether to accept a settlement or go to trial. Below are (A) focused questions to ask your attorney, (B) a checklist of documents and information to bring to the conference, (C) a framework for evaluating settlement offers (including a simple decision test), (D) practical settlement-term items to negotiate and watch for, and (E) next-step planning and negotiation tactics. You can use the short list of “must-ask” questions at the top in the meeting, and keep the rest for follow-up.

A — Must-ask questions to your attorney (start here)
- What is your recommendation: accept a settlement now, make a counteroffer, or prepare for trial? Why?  
- Based on the facts and evidence, what is my realistic chance of success at trial (percentage range) and what are the likely outcomes/remedies the court could order?  
- What are the best/worst/most likely results if we go to trial? How do those compare to the current settlement offer?  
- What are the total estimated costs if we go to trial (attorney fees, court costs, expert fees, time off work, moving costs, etc.)?  
- How long until the trial would occur and how long might it take (timeline)?  
- If we accept settlement, what exactly will be entered (consent judgment, dismissal, payment plan, vacate date)? Will that create a public eviction/judgment record?  
- Does accepting any settlement affect the custody or support issues (now or in future family court proceedings)?  
- Can we include terms protecting my custody/support position (non-admission clause, confidentiality, or language preserving my rights)?  
- If we go to trial and win, what are the realistic options for collecting any judgment (eviction enforcement, garnishment, wage withholding)? If we lose, what are likely consequences (eviction, monetary judgment, impact on housing)?  
- If I need time to decide at the conference, can we get time to consult and present a counteroffer later? How much time would you recommend?  
- If the other side makes a lowball settlement, what would be a reasonable counteroffer range to propose?  
- Are settlement negotiations confidential or admissible later? How does Hennepin County handle settlement conference communications?  
- What contingency or “safety” terms should we insist on (e.g., phased move-out, payment plan, release language, attorney-fee shift)?  
- What is our bottom line — the worst acceptable settlement you would recommend I accept? Conversely, what would you consider a “walk-away” offer?  
- How will you keep me informed during the settlement conference? Can I be present/consult by phone?  
- If we accept a settlement, can you draft/approve the written agreement before I sign it?

B — Documents and notes to bring to the settlement conference
- Lease or rental agreement; notices (notice to vacate, pay or quit, etc.)  
- Court pleadings and all prior orders in this case (complaint, answer, hearing orders)  
- All written communications with the respondent (texts, emails, letters) and dates/times of phone calls or in-person conversations (timeline)  
- Rent payment records, bank statements, receipts, records of deposits or security deposit accounting  
- Photos, videos, repair estimates, inspection reports, list of property condition issues and dates  
- Witness names and short statements (neighbors, property manager, co-tenants)  
- Any custody/support orders, case numbers, and documentation showing how tenancy dispute relates to custody or support (if relevant)  
- Prior settlement offers (dates/amounts) and your notes on what you’re willing to give/need to get  
- Budget spreadsheet showing current finances, costs of moving, and what you can realistically pay if a payment plan is proposed

C — Framework to evaluate a settlement offer (simple decision test)
1) Identify the offer terms (exact language): money, move-out date, releases, confidentiality, judgment entry, who pays costs/fees.  
2) Estimate expected trial outcome (monetary + non-monetary) and probabilities (best / likely / worst).  
3) Estimate additional costs and time for going to trial.  
4) Calculate a simple expected value (EV): EV = (probability of winning × likely award) − expected additional costs. Compare EV to settlement amount.  
5) Adjust EV for non-monetary factors: eviction record, impact on custody, stress, lost time, and risk tolerance.  
If settlement amount ≥ EV (after adjustments and accounting for non-monetary impacts) — leaning to accept; if less than EV and you’re willing to take the risk and pay costs, go to trial. Ask your attorney to run these numbers with their legal probability estimate.

D — Settlement terms and clauses to negotiate / watch for
- Exact dollar amount and payment schedule (dates, interest, consequences for missed payments).  
- Move-out date and condition of premises (cleaning, repairs, security deposit disposition).  
- Whether the agreement will create a judgment or be a dismissal upon payment/performance. If judgment, how will it be satisfied/recorded?  
- Mutual release vs unilateral release (who is released from claims). Make sure release doesn’t unintentionally waive custody/support rights.  
- No-admission/non-liability language (says the settlement is not an admission of wrongdoing).  
- Attorney-fee allocation (who pays fees; cap on fees).  
- Confidentiality or non-disparagement clauses (if important for custody or reputation).  
- Enforcement remedies and remedies for breach (liquidated damages, re-entry, immediate judgment).  
- Right to seek court approval or the ability to seek enforcement in the same case.  
- A clause preserving ability to challenge or renegotiate if there are misrepresentations.  
- Signature and effective date, and who will file the dismissal or judgment satisfaction.

Red flags: any term that waives future custody/support claims, broad general releases that could affect family law rights, ambiguous payment schedules, or language that automatically admits fault.

E — Practical negotiation tactics and next steps
- Ask your attorney to give you a specific bottom line (minimum acceptable) and an ideal settlement. Put that in writing or confirm in email.  
- Request the attorney present the settlement to you in writing before you sign. Never sign a verbal-only settlement.  
- If custody/support issues are intertwined, consider coordinating with your family-law counsel (if different) so settlement language won’t hurt those matters.  
- If the other side is offering phased concessions (e.g., clearance of arrears in exchange for vacating), ask for escrowed payments or consent judgment that’s satisfied on compliance.  
- Use “time to consider” as leverage: don’t feel compelled to accept on the spot. Ask for a short window to call your attorney.  
- If you want to keep options open, consider a temporary agreement (short-term extension, payment plan) while preparing for trial.  
- If you accept, require the other side to file the settlement or dismissal with the court promptly and confirm how the record will show the resolution.  
- Prepare witnesses and evidence as if for trial even if settlement is likely — this strengthens your bargaining position.

Sample short script you can use at the meeting or call:
- “Robert, can you explain in plain terms: what you think will happen at trial, the likelihood of success, the timeline and full cost, and whether this settlement preserves my custody/support rights? What is our bottom line and what would you recommend I do right now?”

Final tips
- Ask your attorney for probabilities and to run the EV calculation with you.  
- Verify whether the settlement conference is confidential under local rules and whether offers are admissible later. Ask Robert to explain Hennepin County practice for settlement conferences.  
- If you want, I can: (a) draft a one-page checklist you can bring to the conference, (b) draft an email to Robert summarizing these questions and asking for his recommendation, or (c) create a decision worksheet you can use when an offer is made. Which would you like next?

