Response (FULL, NOT TRUNCATED):
Good — organizing questions ahead of the settlement conference and trial is smart. Below I’ve put together a practical, prioritized checklist you can use when talking with Robert Kim (and at the conference). It’s split into: what to ask about strategy (settle vs. trial), details to negotiate in any settlement, trial-prep and evidence checklist, custody/support-specific questions, a simple way to evaluate settlement offers, and suggested next steps (including a short script/email you can send to your lawyer). Tell me if you want any piece turned into an email or into a one-page worksheet you can bring to the conference.

1) Core strategic questions to ask your attorney (high priority)
- What are the strengths and weaknesses of our case (facts and law)? If we go to trial, what issues is the court likely to find for/against us?
- How does the civil-division eviction standard apply to our facts in Hennepin County? Are there procedural defenses or technical issues that could get the case dismissed?
- What’s the realistic likelihood (percent estimate) of winning at trial on the eviction claims? On any monetary claims (rent, fees)?
- What damages/relief could we realistically obtain at trial (possession only, money judgment for rent, damages, attorney’s fees)?
- What are the likely outcomes if we lose at trial (eviction timeline, money judgment, collection methods like wage garnishment)?
- What are the costs of going to trial (additional attorney fees, court costs, time, personal stress) vs. the costs of settling?
- Are there statutory or case-law rules specific to Minnesota/Hennepin County that could affect our case (e.g., notice requirements, procedural protections)?
- Are there any upcoming deadlines (discovery, pretrial filings, motions, deadlines for submitting settlement offers) I must meet?
- Do we have any leverage (witnesses, documents, violations by landlord/respondent, procedural missteps) that could improve settlement terms?
- If we settle, what will that mean for the eviction record and my ability to rent later? Can an eviction be sealed or expunged in Minnesota?
- If we accept a settlement, could that be used adversely in the custody/support parts of the case? Should family-court counsel be consulted before settling?
- If we go to trial and win, what’s the enforcement process and timeline for getting possession or collecting money? How easy/hard is enforcement?
- If settlement fails at the conference, what’s the immediate next step? Will the trial date still go forward?

2) Settlement terms you should confirm/ask for (items to negotiate and have in writing)
- Possession: exact move-out date or confirmation that you keep possession until X date.
- Payments: amount, schedule, and whether payments will satisfy all claims (list what’s covered—back rent, fees, repairs).
- Dismissal/Release: whether the landlord/respondent will release all current claims (and whether you release counterclaims).
- Eviction record: explicit agreement re: not reporting to tenant-screening services or filing a satisfaction/vacate of judgment; language for removal of eviction from public records if applicable.
- Confidentiality/non-disparagement clause (if you want one).
- Judgment language: whether a money judgment will be entered (and whether it will be stayed or vacated after compliance).
- Costs and fees: who pays court costs and attorney fees.
- References: written neutral rental reference clause.
- Enforcement remedy: what happens if the other party breaches the settlement (automatic judgment? Short notice to cure?).
- Child custody/support impact: clause clarifying that the settlement does/does not impact custody or child support claims.
- Signatures and filing: confirm that the settlement will be filed with the court as a stipulation or order and exactly how that will be handled.
- Time to reconsider: short period to review the written settlement before signing (if desired).

3) Trial-prep and evidence checklist (what to gather and confirm)
- Core documents: lease, notices (termination, rent demand), receipts, canceled checks, bank statements showing payments, communication (texts, emails), repair requests and responses, photos, inspection reports.
- Witnesses list: names, contact info, summary of expected testimony (neighbors, roommates, maintenance staff).
- Timeline of events: concise chronology you can give the attorney and the court.
- Records related to custody/support: court orders, child support calculations, payment records — know what parts might be used cross-purpose.
- Any prior judgments involving either party (eviction history).
- Police reports, medical records, or other third-party documentation if relevant to habitability or safety.
- Proof of attempts to mitigate (if you paid or tried to resolve issues) — shows reasonableness.
- Exhibits and proposed witness questions for trial; ask your attorney to help prepare a short exhibit list.
- Discovery status: what you still need to request (documents, interrogatories, depositions, subpoenas).

4) Custody and support – questions to ask specifically
- Does settling the eviction case have any direct legal effect on the custody/support proceedings? Could it be used as evidence?
- Should I consult family-court counsel before finalizing settlement to protect custody/support interests?
- If settlement requires payments, will that affect child-support calculations or be applied by the family court?
- If the landlord/respondent is the same person involved in custody/support, how do we avoid cross-use of settlement terms or admissions?
- Are there confidentiality clauses we should insist on to prevent settlement being used in family court?

5) Risk, cost and benefit assessment (a quick decision framework)
- Estimate the value if you win at trial (possession + amount of money you might recover).
- Estimate what you could lose if you lose at trial (eviction date, money judgment, extra costs).
- Estimate attorney costs and time to trial and enforcement.
- Compare: Expected value of trial = (probability of winning) * (benefit if win) – (probability of losing) * (cost if lose) – (trial costs). If the expected value of settlement is higher or less risky given your tolerance, settling may be preferable.
- Also factor non-monetary considerations: ability to stay in housing, credit/tenant screening effects, stress, effect on custody/support.

6) Negotiation tactics and things to tell your attorney beforehand
- Give Robert a bottom-line BATNA (best alternative to negotiated agreement): the worst offer you’ll accept and what you absolutely need (e.g., stay through school year, move-out date, rent forgiveness amount).
- Ask the lawyer to propose an initial settlement demand anchored at a favorable number; ask what counter-offers to expect and a script for the conference.
- Ask whether your attorney recommends offering a conditional settlement (e.g., “I’ll pay X if you file a satisfaction and withdraw eviction within Y days”).
- Ask whether it’s better for the attorney to negotiate or to have you speak in the settlement conference.

7) Procedural / scheduling questions to confirm
- Who will attend the settlement conference? Will the judge or a settlement officer mediate? Will the respondent and their counsel be present?
- How long is the conference scheduled for and what is the typical format in Hennepin County civil division?
- If settlement is reached verbally at the conference, what is the process to make it binding? How quickly must it be reduced to writing?
- If settlement fails, when will trial be scheduled and what are the next deadlines?

8) Post-settlement and enforcement questions
- If settlement requires the other side to do something (vacate, pay), how do we enforce it? Do we get an agreed order or judgment that’s easier to enforce?
- What additional costs might enforcement involve (motions, contempt, collection)?
- If the verdict/settlement is unfavorable, what are appeal options and timelines?

9) Practical and personal considerations to discuss
- How long can I afford to stay in the litigation process (financially and emotionally)?
- Are there housing alternatives if the eviction is finalized (emergency resources, local tenant support in Hennepin County)?
- Timing with custody/support matters — do we need to align calendar events or avoid certain outcomes before family court hearings?

10) Suggested short script/email to send to Robert Kim (you can paste/edit)
Subject: Questions before settlement conference — eviction case (civil division)

Hi Robert,
Our settlement conference is coming up and I want to be fully prepared. I’d like to discuss the following before the conference:
- Your assessment of our strengths/weaknesses and the realistic likelihood of success at trial
- The likely outcomes (possession/money) if we win or lose and enforcement timelines
- Recommended settlement terms (what to ask for) and my BATNA (I can accept no less than ________)
- Whether settling could affect the custody/support issues and whether we should consult family-court counsel
- What evidence/documents you recommend I bring and any witness prep
- The Hennepin County conference format and what to expect procedurally

Could we meet/call for 20–30 minutes before the conference? I’ll bring a timeline and the documents listed here.

Thanks,
[Your name]

11) What I can do next for you
- Turn this into a one-page checklist or printable to bring to the conference.
- Draft the actual email to Robert or a call script.
- Help you prepare a settlement evaluation worksheet with numbers you can plug in (probabilities, amounts).
- Help assemble a prioritized list of documents and create a clean timeline for the attorney.

If you want, tell me:
- Any specific settlement offers already made (amounts, move-out dates, terms), and
- What your non-negotiables are (e.g., must avoid formal eviction on record, must stay until school year ends, maximum you can pay).

I’ll use that to make a tailored recommendation and a short decision worksheet to use during negotiations.

